WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
STEPHANIE WONG: In
the last episode,

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.240
we explained how
the GCP global load

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.370
balancer is able
to overflow traffic

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.410
to back in instance
scripts in the next closest

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.570
region during traffic spikes.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.970
Not only is this more
reliable for Beyond Treats'

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.690
growing online business, but
it also gives dog parents

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.695
a better overall experience with
lower overall latency risks.

00:00:18.695 --> 00:00:20.820
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: But the
Beyond Treats leadership

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.220
is hesitant, and they
want to save cost

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.690
in as many places as possible.

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.830
They have a new claim to fame
after being featured on TV,

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.259
and are new to serving online
customers around the world.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:35.190
Will global load balancing give
them a noticeable difference

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.020
in performance versus regional?

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.320
STEPHANIE WONG: Today, we're
going to find that out.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.785
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.140
What we'll be creating today
is a simple test web server

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:53.510
running a CPU-intensive
application

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.670
that computes Mandelbrot sets.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.740
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: What
are Mandelbrot sets?

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.198
STEPHANIE WONG: A
Mandelbrot set is

00:00:59.198 --> 00:01:01.220
generated by computational
iteration, which

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:04.280
means to repeat a process
over and over again.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.100
And that will be a Python script
we install on our load test

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:07.900
instances.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:08.300
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Huh.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:08.800
OK.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:09.830
I'm intrigued.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.955
STEPHANIE WONG: We'll then
measure network capacity

00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:14.300
using load testing tools
on one VM instance.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.550
Next, we'll add a single
region load balancer

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.480
with back ends in
the same region,

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:19.938
and then measure
the response time.

00:01:19.938 --> 00:01:22.272
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: And then
let me guess, after that, we

00:01:22.272 --> 00:01:23.910
will test the
performance when we scale

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.540
the network to multiple regions
using global load balancing,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.570
and then compare that
with the performance using

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.400
a single regional load balancer.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:31.600
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.360
Ding, ding, ding!

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:32.860
Exactly.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:33.985
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Awesome.

00:01:33.985 --> 00:01:34.880
I am super excited.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.450
Let's do it.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:36.825
Let's go through
the ingredients.

00:01:36.825 --> 00:01:37.658
STEPHANIE WONG: Yes.

00:01:37.658 --> 00:01:38.450
Here they are.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:41.630
You'll need load testing
tools like siege and httperf.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.090
Siege simulates requests from
a specified number of users,

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.180
so you can see the capacity
and expected response

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.040
times for an application.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:52.250
httperf sends a specific
number of requests per second,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.490
regardless of whether responses
or errors are received.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.418
It tells you how
applications respond to load.

00:01:57.418 --> 00:01:59.960
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: You also need
a single [INAUDIBLE] machine

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.410
instance back end, a single
region load balancer,

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.240
and a single region
managed instance group.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.440
And finally, a global load
balancer with multi-regional

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.180
managed instance groups.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.760
STEPHANIE WONG: To start, create
a VPC network for testing,

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.490
then define a firewall rule
to allow internal traffic

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.370
and allow SSH
traffic to the VPC.

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:21.110
Now, lets determine
the serving capacity

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.980
of a single VM instance.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.270
Create a VM called web
server with four CPUs.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.690
Include a startup script that
installs the Mandelbrot server.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.600
Create a firewall rule to
allow external access to it,

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.890
and then grab the VM
public IP address.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.550
Enter it in your browser, and
you get the computed Mandelbrot

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:38.480
set.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.030
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Then create
a second VM in the same zone

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.660
called load test.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.160
[? Text ?] ssh into
the load test instance

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.810
and install siege and httperf.

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.000
And try to request
around 20 requests

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.550
from four parallel users.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:56.990
You should get an output of
about 5.05 requests per second.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:01.700
Using httperf, run 500 requests
at the rate of four requests

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:04.490
per second, and repeat
it at different rates,

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:07.995
like 5, 7, and 10
requests per second.

00:03:07.995 --> 00:03:09.620
You will see that
the system can handle

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.290
five requests per second,
and then drastically

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:15.080
jumps in response time
and connection errors

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.130
when you have 7 or 10
connections per second.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.390
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, now let's
put a single region load

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:22.430
balancer in front with
the managed instance

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:24.350
group in the same region.

00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.300
Once you create the
https load balancer

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.360
with three VMs in the same
region using a managed instance

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.780
template, and health checks,
you can run the same load test

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:34.670
against the load balance system.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:37.130
As a load increases
past serving capacity,

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:41.090
the average and maximum request
latency increases sharply.

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:43.820
With 10 requests per second,
the average request latency

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.930
is close to 500 milliseconds.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.290
But with 20 requests
per second, the latency

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.700
is 5,000 requests per second.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.070
It's a tenfold increase.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.020
Your dog fans are
not going to be happy

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:56.540
when you have a flash buy
one get one free sale on dog

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:57.725
birthday cakes.

00:03:57.725 --> 00:03:59.600
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now,
here's the real test.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.900
Let's set up a global web
server with https load balancing

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.840
with back ends deployed
in multiple regions.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.490
Once you create a
managed instance

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:12.230
group, in second region, with
a maximum capacity of 10 RPS,

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:16.430
run 500 requests at 10 RPS,
and you will get the results.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.760
After the system stabilizes,
the average response time

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:23.030
is around 400
milliseconds, at 10 RPS,

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.990
and a minor increase to 700
milliseconds, at 20 RPS.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.450
Seems like the
discrepancy is a lot less

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:31.280
than the 5,000
milliseconds delay

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.415
we saw with the
regional load balancer.

00:04:33.415 --> 00:04:35.540
STEPHANIE WONG: In our
regional load balancer test,

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.330
we saw the load balancer
became overloaded when traffic

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.810
increased past capacity.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.352
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
This is what tends

00:04:41.352 --> 00:04:43.430
to happen with typical
on-premise load balancers,

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:47.100
or when using the standard tier
network on regional https load

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.020
balancers.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:49.562
STEPHANIE WONG: The
request latencies

00:04:49.562 --> 00:04:51.230
increased by more than 10x.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.660
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Meanwhile,
using the global https load

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.220
balancing setup lets
your traffic overflow

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:00.080
to the next closest region with
available serving capacity.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.660
You have lower increase
in end user latency,

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.405
and a better user
experience, meaning--

00:05:04.405 --> 00:05:06.530
STEPHANIE WONG: Beyond
Treats' customers will still

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:09.830
be able to order pumpkin pie,
flax buscuits, peanut butter

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.470
treats, and carrot cakes
on National Dog Day.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.625
No full service outages
when traffic spikes.

00:05:14.625 --> 00:05:16.250
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
For more information

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.200
about GCP's global
load balancing

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.830
optimizing application latency,
check out the documentation

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:23.040
in the link below.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.980
STEPHANIE WONG: That's all for
today on Get Cooking in Cloud.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.563
Here's hoping you are whipping
up your own capacity management

00:05:27.563 --> 00:05:29.180
with Cloud load balancers.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:30.230
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: If
you liked this video

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:31.920
and would like to see
more such content,

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.580
don't forget to like and
subscribe to our channel.

